    </header>
    <div type="book" osisID="Jas">
      <chapter osisID="Jas.1">
        <verse osisID="Jas.1.1" n="1">“James (Yaakov), a servant of Elohim and the Lord Yeshua the Messiah. To the Twelve Tribes scattered in all places, joyful greetings.”</verse>
        <verse osisID="Jas.1.2" n="2">“My beloved brothers, consider this joy if you fall into temptations.”</verse>
        <verse osisID="Jas.1.3" n="3">“And knowing that your emunah (faith) if it is complete, produces burdens.”<note type="footnote" n="12">See Exodus 6:7. Can also mean, “burden, forced labor, compulsory service, burden bearing.”</note></verse>
        <verse osisID="Jas.1.4" n="4">“But the burdens are necessary until the end, until you continue to be fully successful; you will not lack absolutely nothing of you.</verse>
        <verse osisID="Jas.1.5" n="5">“But, if one among you lacks wisdom, this one should pray for what is given to all mankind; then also he gives to him.”</verse>
        <verse osisID="Jas.1.6" n="6">“But he should pray with emunah (faith) and not in doubt. For whoever doubts this, he is similar to balances on the sea.”</verse>
        <verse osisID="Jas.1.7" n="7">“And this man should not think that he will receive anything from the Lord.”</verse>
        <verse osisID="Jas.1.8" n="8">“And a doubter is unstable in all his ways.”</verse>
        <verse osisID="Jas.1.9" n="9">“So, let every lowly brother boast in his elevation;”</verse>
        <verse osisID="Jas.1.10" n="10">“but the rich should rejoice of his lowering: because like a flower of the field it dries out.</verse>
        <verse osisID="Jas.1.11" n="11">“And like a flower that goes out and is cut off; it is a fleeing shadow and does not stand.”</verse>
        <verse osisID="Jas.1.12" n="12">“And happy is the man whom Eloah corrects and does not throw away the reproof nor become fed up. For after the sufferings, you will receive the crown life that Yehovah’s promises for those who are loved by Him.”</verse>
        <verse osisID="Jas.1.13" n="13">“Let not one man say that if temptation comes upon him, this comes from Yehovah. Because Yehovah is not put to the test or does to mankind for evil; for He is not testing anyone.”</verse>
        <verse osisID="Jas.1.14" n="14">“Only anyone is tested if his lust defeats him.”</verse>
        <verse osisID="Jas.1.15" n="15">“Then if lust is desired, it causes sin. But sin, if not restrained, it causes death.”</verse>
        <verse osisID="Jas.1.16" n="16">“Do not be led astray, beloved brothers.”</verse>
        <verse osisID="Jas.1.17" n="17">“Every good gift comes from above, from the light of the Father. And with Him there is no exchange of light and darkness.”</verse>
        <verse osisID="Jas.1.18" n="18">“And He revealed to us, according to His will by way of His Word of Truth, that we are first born of His creation.<note type="footnote" n="17">Literally, “fat, or fattened.”</note>”</verse>
        <verse osisID="Jas.1.19" n="19">“For this, beloved brothers, every son of man must be quick to hear but not speak and not to anger.”</verse>
        <verse osisID="Jas.1.20" n="20">“For the anger from the sons of man does not produce what is good before Yehovah.”</verse>
        <verse osisID="Jas.1.21" n="21"/>
        <verse osisID="Jas.1.22" n="22" alt:num="Jas.1.21">“But be of those who do the word, and not hearers only, who swindle you.”</verse>
        <verse osisID="Jas.1.23" n="23" alt:num="Jas.1.22">“For if there is one that hears the Word, but does not do it, he is like a man who looks at himself in the tinted mirrors.”</verse>
        <verse osisID="Jas.1.24" n="24" alt:num="Jas.1.23">“Because after seeing himself, he leaves it and forgets what he saw.”</verse>
        <verse osisID="Jas.1.25" n="25" alt:num="Jas.1.24">“But whoever sees the decree of joy, and he performs it, and does not forget what he heard, but does it, he will be holy in all his doings.”</verse>
        <verse osisID="Jas.1.26" n="26" alt:num="Jas.1.25">“But if one of you thinks he is a servant of Yehovah and does not guard his tongue in the bridle only inflames his own heart; this service of Yehovah is not good.”</verse>
        <verse osisID="Jas.1.27" n="27" alt:num="Jas.1.26">“And good service without lack Yehovah the Father, it is he who goes out to strengthen the orphans and the widows in their sorrows.”</verse>
      </chapter>
      <chapter osisID="Jas.2">
        <verse osisID="Jas.2.1" n="1">“Beloved brothers, consider the faith of Yeshua the Messiah, our Lord, who does not beguile a person.”</verse>
        <verse osisID="Jas.2.2" n="2">“Because if one comes to the Beit Din<note type="footnote" n="20">Mishnaic term first found in the Mishnah Demai 7:3 and means, “house of judgment.”</note> with a gold ring and nice clothes, and also a poor person in bad clothes;”</verse>
        <verse osisID="Jas.2.3" n="3">“you see upon the exalted one’s pleasant garments and say to him, "Sit with us, for it is good;" But you say to the poor, "Stand there or sit at our feet."</verse>
        <verse osisID="Jas.2.4" n="4">“And don’t scrutinize well regarding this, just simply judge and make a bad judgment.”</verse>
        <verse osisID="Jas.2.5" n="5">“Hear me, beloved brothers! Did not Yehovah choose the poor in this world - rich in faith, so those loved by Him will inherit the promise?”</verse>
        <verse osisID="Jas.2.6" n="6">“However, you shame the poor. And the rich: aren’t they the ones doing everything in their strength against you by force, then take and judge you?”</verse>
        <verse osisID="Jas.2.7" n="7">“And don’t they curse and blaspheme the good name from which you are called?”</verse>
        <verse osisID="Jas.2.8" n="8">“If you are doing the law according to the writings in the Torah and love your companions as yourself, you do good.”</verse>
        <verse osisID="Jas.2.9" n="9">“But if you see that a man commits a sin and you punish according to the law as like a transgressor,”</verse>
        <verse osisID="Jas.2.10" n="10">“for if one upholds all of the Torah entirely and has one sin, he is guilty<note type="footnote" n="21">Mishnaic term first found in the Mishnah Berakhot (c. 190 - 230 CE).</note> for all of it.”</verse>
        <verse osisID="Jas.2.11" n="11">“Because the one who said, “You shall not commit adultery;” did He not also say, “You shall not murder.” And if you do not commit adultery but commit murder, you are transgressing the law.”</verse>
        <verse osisID="Jas.2.12" n="12">“When you are able to understand what is spoken and act accordingly, these are judged by the law in joyfulness.”</verse>
        <verse osisID="Jas.2.13" n="13">“But there will be a bad judgment for the one who does not show mercy and the mercy boasts on the side of judgment.”</verse>
        <verse osisID="Jas.2.14" n="14">“And what benefit is there, beloved brothers, if one says he has faith and does not have works? For faith has no ability to sanctify him.”</verse>
        <verse osisID="Jas.2.15" n="15" alt:num="Jas.2.15-Jas.2.16">“And if there is a brother or sister who lacks support each and every day,” and one of you say to them giving words of comfort of Yehovah to fill them, but does not give anything to them for the benefit of the body; what benefit do these things have for them?” KJV verse 2:15: If a brother or sister be naked, and destitute of daily food, KJV verse 2:16: and one of you say unto them, Depart in peace, be ye warmed and filled; notwithstanding ye give them not those things which are needful to the body; what doth it profit?</verse>
        <verse osisID="Jas.2.16" n="16" alt:num="Jas.2.17">“So is faith if without works.” KJV verse 2:17: “Even so faith, if it hath not works, is dead, being alone.”</verse>
        <verse osisID="Jas.2.17" n="17" alt:num="Jas.2.18">“But one could say, I have faith and does not have works also. I testify to you regarding my faith and my works.” KJV verse 2:18: “Yea, a man may say, thou hast faith, and I have works: shew me thy faith without thy works, and I will shew thee my faith by my works.”</verse>
        <verse osisID="Jas.2.18" n="18" alt:num="Jas.2.19">“You have faith that Yehovah is One - you do good. The satans also have faith and tremble.” KJV verse 2:19: “Thou believest that there is one God; thou doest well: the devils also believe, and tremble.”</verse>
        <verse osisID="Jas.2.19" n="19" alt:num="Jas.2.20">“But you should desire to know that faith without works is dead.” KJV verse 2:20: “But wilt thou know, O vain man, that faith without works is dead?”</verse>
        <verse osisID="Jas.2.20" n="20" alt:num="Jas.2.21">“Was not our father Abrahan, justified by his actions when he brought his son as a sacrifice up the altar?” KJV verse 2:21: “Was not Abraham our father justified by works, when he had offered Isaac his son upon the altar?”</verse>
        <verse osisID="Jas.2.21" n="21" alt:num="Jas.2.22">“And by this, you are able to see faith operating by its works; and by works creates complete faith.” KJV verse 2:22: “Seest thou how faith wrought with his works, and by works was faith made perfect?”</verse>
        <verse osisID="Jas.2.22" n="22" alt:num="Jas.2.23">“And in this, that which is said in the Torah is fulfilled. And Abraham believed and it was considered to him as righteousness.” KJV verse 2:23: And the scripture was fulfilled which saith, Abraham believed God, and it was imputed unto him for righteousness: and he was called the Friend of God.</verse>
        <verse osisID="Jas.2.23" n="23" alt:num="Jas.2.24">“And by his works, you are able to see that a man is justified by his works and not by faith alone.” KJV verse 2:24: “Ye see then how that by works a man is justified, and not by faith only.”</verse>
        <verse osisID="Jas.2.24" n="24" alt:num="Jas.2.25">“And similarly to Rahab the harlot. Is she not justified by the works since she took the spies and hid them?” KJV verse 2:25: “Likewise also was not Rahab the harlot justified by works, when she had received the messengers, and had sent them out another way?”</verse>
        <verse osisID="Jas.2.25" n="25" alt:num="Jas.2.26">“Because just as the body without the spirit is dead, so faith without works is dead.” KJV verse 2:26: “For as the body without the spirit is dead, so faith without works is dead also.”</verse>
      </chapter>
      <chapter osisID="Jas.3">
        <verse osisID="Jas.3.1" n="1">“Beloved brothers, don’t any one of you be burdened to teach; but know that the penalty will be greater.”</verse>
        <verse osisID="Jas.3.2" n="2">“Because we sin in many things, and he who has not sinned in anything is a holy man and is able to control all the body.”</verse>
        <verse osisID="Jas.3.3" n="3">“But see that we lead horses with the bridle in order to lead them according to our desire.”</verse>
        <verse osisID="Jas.3.4" n="4">“And moreover, ships, although great, move strongly like the winds, albeit they are led by way of a small helm; which is in the hand of the Messenger.”</verse>
        <verse osisID="Jas.3.5" n="5">“Thus, in the same way, the tongue is a small organ, and achieves great things. See, a small fire is a burner of a great forest.”</verse>
        <verse osisID="Jas.3.6" n="6">“And also, the tongue full of wickedness,<note type="footnote" n="23">Aramaic noun meaning, “wickedness.”</note> is like a fire in the world. Thus, the tongue, in our organs, causes all the body to sin, and reveals us in our walks, as if it is revealed from Gehinnom.”</verse>
        <verse osisID="Jas.3.7" n="7">“Because the nature of all the beasts, fowl and the serpents on dry land or in the sea is like the nature of a man.”</verse>
        <verse osisID="Jas.3.8" n="8">“But the tongue no man is able to bridle, because it is evil and full of the poison of death.”</verse>
        <verse osisID="Jas.3.9" n="9">“By the tongue we praise Yehovah the Father; and by it we curse the man who is in the image of Elohim;”</verse>
        <verse osisID="Jas.3.10" n="10">“from one mouth goes out praises and curses. And it should not be so, beloved brothers.”</verse>
        <verse osisID="Jas.3.11" n="11">“And is there a spring that has salty and sweet water?”</verse>
        <verse osisID="Jas.3.12" n="12">“Or is there a fig tree that could give<note type="footnote" n="24">Mishnaic verb form that is a result of Aramism with the added yod (י).</note> oil, or a vine, figs? Therefore, the spring will not give both salty and fresh water.”</verse>
        <verse osisID="Jas.3.13" n="13">“Who that is clever and wise among you, demonstrates to me his good walk in wisdom.”</verse>
        <verse osisID="Jas.3.14" n="14">“But if there is jealousy and hatred in your hearts, do not boast and do not deceive as a witness of the truth.”</verse>
        <verse osisID="Jas.3.15" n="15">“Because this is not wisdom that comes from above, but only from the earth, from man and from the satans.”</verse>
        <verse osisID="Jas.3.16" n="16">“Because those who have jealousy and hatred have many bad things.”</verse>
        <verse osisID="Jas.3.17" n="17">“However, wisdom is from above: from the beginning: pure, and afterwards, shalom, then rest, then to be able to quickly appease<note type="footnote" n="25">Aramaic Peal verb binyan meaning, “to appease or to reconcile.”</note>, and to fully hear of mercy, and lastly, for good and not burdened within or flattery.”</verse>
        <verse osisID="Jas.3.18" n="18">“But the fruits of righteousness are sown in peace, for those who make peace.”</verse>
      </chapter>
      <chapter osisID="Jas.4">
        <verse osisID="Jas.4.1" n="1">“And from what comes from battles among you? Are they not for your beliefs fighting in your organs?”</verse>
        <verse osisID="Jas.4.2" n="2">“Then you desire and do not receive, and you retaliate and are vengeful and you do not gain from it; But all because of what? Because you do not pray!”</verse>
        <verse osisID="Jas.4.3" n="3">“And you make a prayer and do not receive an answer, because you pray with evil.”</verse>
        <verse osisID="Jas.4.4" n="4">“The Adulterer and the adulteress: do you not know that whoever loves this world is a hater of Yehovah, and whomever wants to be loved by this world will hate Yehovah.”</verse>
        <verse osisID="Jas.4.5" n="5">“So, you thought that the Torah said for naught? The spirit that dwells within you is lusting against Yehovah commandments,”</verse>
        <verse osisID="Jas.4.6" n="6">“and gives a lot of grace because the Torah said that Yehovah elevates the lowly and humbles the lofty.”</verse>
        <verse osisID="Jas.4.7" n="7">“And now be submitted to Yehovah, and fight against the satans, and they<note type="footnote" n="26">This appears to be a spelling error for the tav ( ת) should be a yod ( י) for the third common plural, meaning “they.” As spelled, this is translated as, “You will flee” and is nonsensical. .</note> will flee from you.”</verse>
        <verse osisID="Jas.4.8" n="8">“So draw near to Yehovah, and He will draw near to you. And purify your sinful hands and your heart will be made clean.”</verse>
        <verse osisID="Jas.4.9" n="9">“And you will endure hardships. Weep, for your worries will become weeping and your joy to sorrow.<note type="footnote" n="27">Aramaic masculine noun meaning, “sorrow or mourning.” The lamed (ל) is serving as a direct object marker which is a second Temple Hebrew grammar marker.</note>”</verse>
        <verse osisID="Jas.4.10" n="10">“Humble yourselves in the presence of Yehovah; He will hear you.”</verse>
        <verse osisID="Jas.4.11" n="11">“Beloved brothers do not go slandering<note type="footnote" n="28">Aramaic feminine noun meaning, “slander.”</note> among yourselves, because he that goes slandering his brother is he who slanders the law. And if you do so, you are not fulfilling the law.”</verse>
        <verse osisID="Jas.4.12" n="12">“And there is only One who gives the law that is able to punish and pardon. Who are you to judge others?”</verse>
        <verse osisID="Jas.4.13" n="13">“And now if you say today or tomorrow, we want to go<note type="footnote" n="29">Mishnaic word first found in Mishnah Eruvin 3:1 (c. 190 - 230 CE).</note> to that place or that city. Or we want to stand there a year to do work negotiating and profiting,”</verse>
        <verse osisID="Jas.4.14" n="14">“You do not know what tomorrow will be, for what are your lives? Are they not a spirit for a short time, and following this, it is gone?”</verse>
        <verse osisID="Jas.4.15" n="15">“And we and you should say, “As Yehovah wills it,” and we live desiring to do this,”</verse>
        <verse osisID="Jas.4.16" n="16">“But now your boasting is in your glory<note type="footnote" n="30">Aramaic feminine noun meaning, “pride” or “glory."</note> and this is evil;”</verse>
        <verse osisID="Jas.4.17" n="17">“because whoever that knows and does not do this, is considered in sin.”</verse>
      </chapter>
      <chapter osisID="Jas.5">
        <verse osisID="Jas.5.1" n="1">“And now the wealthy cry out and weep about the evil that comes upon you.”</verse>
        <verse osisID="Jas.5.2" n="2">“Your riches decay and your garments are consumed like fire!”</verse>
        <verse osisID="Jas.5.3" n="3">“And your silver and gold rust. This will be a testimony to you!”</verse>
        <verse osisID="Jas.5.4" n="4">“See the deeds of a hired worker at your place who cries out before me. And their cries come before the ears of the Lord of hosts.”</verse>
        <verse osisID="Jas.5.5" n="5">“For you had had lusts of this world and rejoiced at the voice of the seduction.”</verse>
        <verse osisID="Jas.5.6" n="6">“And you were compelled to justify and kill, and he did not protest<note type="footnote" n="31">Mishnaic word Pi’el verb binyan was first found in Mishnah Nazir 4:6.</note> against you.”</verse>
        <verse osisID="Jas.5.7" n="7">“And now beloved brothers, you have hope for the future. See the owner of the field awaits the fruit of the land. And His love is for the first rain and his pinch.”<note type="footnote" n="32">This word means, “pinch” or “nipping” of a fruit.</note></verse>
        <verse osisID="Jas.5.8" n="8">“So even be strong. Be strong in your hearts, because the future of the Lord is near!”</verse>
        <verse osisID="Jas.5.9" n="9">“Don’t sigh against yourselves, beloved brothers, so that you don’t get into trouble because the Judge stands before the door.”</verse>
        <verse osisID="Jas.5.10" n="10">“Beloved brothers, take for you, for instance, the sorrow and the causes from the prophets who spoke in the name of the Lord.”</verse>
        <verse osisID="Jas.5.11" n="11">“See we praise those who suffered. And the reason is from Job, you heard and the end from the Lord you saw because the Lord is merciful and has great kindness.”</verse>
        <verse osisID="Jas.5.12" n="12">“But first of all, beloved brothers, do not swear by the heavens or the earth or any oath. But let your speech be “yes” “yes” and not “no,” so that you do not fail.”</verse>
        <verse osisID="Jas.5.13" n="13">“And if one of you suffers something, he should pray. Or if one of you has a good heart, he should sing (zamer).<note type="footnote" n="33">This is a unique Hebrew marker word meaning “might, song, and pruning.” This word can be compared to the singing done by David when he sang for King Saul and the demons fled (2 Samuel 23:1). Zamar (רמז) is the power of God in the song.</note></verse>
        <verse osisID="Jas.5.14" n="14">“And if anyone is sick, you should call the elders of the community, and they will pray for him and anoint him with oil in the name of the Lord.”</verse>
        <verse osisID="Jas.5.15" n="15">“Then praying in faith will help the sick and the Lord will raise him up. And if he has sin, the Lord will forgive his sins.”</verse>
        <verse osisID="Jas.5.16" n="16">“So, confess your sins one to another, and let each one pray for his companion because Yehovah draws close to all who call upon Him in truth.”</verse>
        <verse osisID="Jas.5.17" n="17">“And also, Elijah the prophet was a man, like us and he prayed that it would not rain upon the earth for three and one-half years.”</verse>
        <verse osisID="Jas.5.18" n="18">“Then again, he prayed that it would rain again, and the heavens gave the rain and the land her fruit.”</verse>
        <verse osisID="Jas.5.19" n="19">“Brothers, if one of you goes from the good path to evil and one withholds him,”</verse>
        <verse osisID="Jas.5.20" n="20">“Know that one who turns him from his wickedness, saves one soul from death and grants atonement from many sins.”</verse>
      </chapter>
    </div>
  </osisText>
</osis>
